title Fold AI -- the whole standing movement, and four tier owners home in one lap
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first and send each finished increment as its own signed round.
think Took the queue's head honestly and then read past it. weigh_the_standing led at twenty-two rungs by fifty-seven lines, and its three siblings -- date_the_standing, herald_the_standing, carry_the_position -- stood immediately behind it at nearly the same weight. Four separate families by the meter's reading.
think Read the harness before choosing scope, and the harness answered the question. ladder_checks.the_standing already calls all four in sequence, in the order the arc grew, with a doc comment explaining why that order is load-bearing. They were never four things; they were one movement the meter could only see one name at a time.
think Hashed every body of all four across caravan/. Each showed the same three-part shape: a wide cohort byte for byte, a small set differing only by the word pub, and exactly one rung differing by reaching its own report directly. Four tier owners -- heed.rye, dwell.rye, swell.rye, endure.rye -- one per body, each answerable by the identity accessor this arc has now written seven times.
think Audited the reach closure for all four at once rather than one at a time. Every symbol they name already stood public in every rung that holds it, and the rungs missing a symbol are missing it exactly where the tier structure says they should be: swell_published and Volume live only from the heralding tier up. So the second hop cost nothing.
think Sorted the reached helpers by whether folding them would move anything. Most are already three-line delegates from earlier laps and were left alone; five were real bodies -- regard_published, swell_published, dwell_note, standing_note, regard_note -- and lifted.
think Stopped to think about the visibility split rather than healing it reflexively. Fold Z taught that a split keyed on the word pub is usually accident; this one is not. A rung reaching the body only through the harness must publish it, since the harness calls rung.weigh_the_standing; a rung calling it itself has no reason to widen its own surface. So each delegate keeps the visibility its body wore -- fold AH's lesson applied to a case where it changes the answer.
think Held every cohort list on disk before appending a line to the harness, which is REDS %111's whole lesson from the lap before, applied rather than remembered.
think Generated the nine harness bodies mechanically from the canonical rung rather than transcribing them -- rewriting each rung-local symbol to rung.X and each same-lap folded symbol to a harness call -- so no line of proven logic passed through a hand.
obs Carry falls 94,151 -> 89,010, a fall of 5,141 -- larger again than fold AH's 4,356, which was itself the arc's largest. It closes with no remainder: 5,780 carried in across the nine families and their visibility twins, 639 carried back out in delegates.
obs Carrying families rose 707 -> 708, and the one is a family born rather than a family split. dwell.rye and swell.rye each held their own private date_the_standing, two distinct texts standing alone; one shared three-line delegate turned two singletons into a carrying pair. A fold that lifts bodies out can still leave a new family behind it.
obs Checks 47, print carried 2,686, and spine 0 stand unmoved for the twelfth fold running. The three named windows remain genuinely orthogonal to the room.
obs The fold cost sixty-four widenings and four accessors born -- more than fold AH's two words, and the reason is legible: this movement's accessors had never been published because no folded body had needed them yet. The ladder pays for each tier once.
obs A movement is a better fold unit than a body. The queue measures one name at a time because that is what a text-keyed meter can see; the harness already knew these four belonged together, and reading it for what it runs together found four families where the queue showed one.
